          "text": "Now I understand the situation. Omarchy quattro migrated from `.conf` files to `.lua` files. The user's previous custom configs (input.conf, bindings.conf, bindings-override.conf) are symlinked into ~/.config/hypr/, but omarchy quattro now loads `input.lua`, `bindings.lua`, etc. instead of the .conf files.\n\nWait, but actually, does the user's custom hyprland.conf still get used? The symlink `hyprland.conf -> dotfiles hyprland.conf` exists, and it sources the .conf files. But quattro also created `hyprland.lua` which takes precedence (Hyprland v0.50+ uses .lua configs).\n\nThe question: user wants their previous input.conf, bindings.conf, bindings-override.conf symlinked to dotfiles — which is already done. But the real issue is likely that omarchy quattro now uses Lua configs, so those .conf files are no longer being loaded. The user wants their customizations to apply.\n\nLet me check what the local .lua files contain (input.lua, bindings.lua) — these are the Quattro-native versions. And check the local bindings.conf vs dotfiles bindings.conf — they differ (local bindings.conf was modified Aug 9? no wait, local one is `-rw-r--r-- 1 himmel himmel 2060 Aug 9 15:37 bindings.conf` — actually that's the symlink target in dotfiles... wait no.\n\nLet me re-read the ls output:\n\n`~/.config/hypr/`:\n- `bindings.conf -> ../../dev/environment/home/.config/hypr/bindings.conf` (symlink to dotfiles)\n- `bindings.lua` (local file, Aug 14)\n- `input.conf -> ../../dev/environment/home/.config/hypr/input.conf` (symlink)\n- `input.lua` (local file, Aug 14)\n- `bindings-override.conf -> dotfiles` (symlink)\n\nDotfiles:\n- bindings.conf (2060 bytes, Aug 9 15:37)\n- bindings-override.conf (10193 bytes)\n- input.conf (1750 bytes, Aug 8)\n\nSo the symlinks DO exist already. The user's request \"sync it (symlinks) with my dotfiles\" is essentially already satisfied. But the user probably doesn't realize that, OR the issue is that quattro now uses Lua files and the .conf files are ignored.\n\nSo the real question: does quattro still read the .conf files? Looking at hyprland.conf (symlinked from dotfiles, but wait — the dotfiles hyprland.conf was modified Aug 14 23:27, 1370 bytes, and it still sources the .conf files).\n\nHmm, actually the dotfiles hyprland.conf is the old-style one. The question is whether Hyprland reads hyprland.lua or hyprland.conf. In Hyprland 0.50+, it tries hyprland.lua first, then falls back to hyprland.conf. So with quattro, hyprland.lua is used, and the .conf files (including input.conf, bindings.conf, bindings-override.conf) are NOT loaded.\n\nBut wait — there are also .lua files in the dotfiles? No, the dotfiles only have .conf files. The local ~/.config/hypr has .lua files.\n\nSo the user's customization is currently being ignored because quattro reads the .lua files, not the .conf files.\n\nOptions:\n1. Sync user's conf customizations into the .lua files (bindings.lua, input.lua), keeping symlinks for the .conf as backup.\n2. Make hyprland.lua require/source the .conf files too.\n3. Use the omarchy skill to understand how quattro handles this.\n\nThe user explicitly says: \"i want my previous hyprland input.conf and bindings.conf and bindings-override.conf and sync it (symlinks) with my dotfiles in this repo\"\n\nSo they want:\n1. The three files symlinked to dotfiles (already done)\n2. Presumably their customizations to take effect in quattro\n\nSince this is about omarchy config, I should load the omarchy skill. The skill says it's REQUIRED for end-user customization of Linux desktop/window manager config, and triggers include Hyprland, keybindings, workspace settings. This matches.\n\nLet me load the omarchy skill first.",
