          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user clarifies: no .conf files used when .lua exists. So the .conf files are indeed dead under quattro. I need to port everything into Lua files in the dotfiles repo and symlink those.\n\nThe task now:\n1. Create `input.lua` in dotfiles — port input.conf settings.\n2. Create `bindings.lua` in dotfiles — port bindings-override.conf (full SUPER→ALT swap) + bindings.conf (custom bindings).\n3. Symlink them from ~/.config/hypr/.\n4. The old .conf symlinks — the user said \"no .conf use on .lua\". Should I remove the .conf symlinks? The user wants their previous configs synced. Since .conf is dead, I could leave the .conf symlinks (harmless) or remove them to avoid confusion. I'll keep them for reference (they asked to sync them), but the real work is the .lua files. Actually, maybe better to ask? No — keep it simple: port to .lua, keep .conf symlinks as-is (they exist and user asked for them), and note it.\n\nNow the big work: writing bindings.lua with the full ALT swap.\n\nKey design decision: how to map the old dispatchers to the new Lua API.\n\nFrom the catalog, the default SUPER bindings are known. For each, I need:\n- hl.unbind(\"SUPER + ...\") — wait, does hl.unbind exist in Hyprland 0.56 Lua API? The skill mentions `hl.unbind(\"SUPER + F\")`. So yes.\n\nBut actually — there might be a smarter way. Since I know the default binding list exactly, I can write a loop-based approach: define a list of keys (the SUPER bindings), and for each, unbind the SUPER version and bind the ALT version. The dispatchers should mirror the defaults (the override.conf mapping). But the defaults' dispatchers use new-style hl.dsp helpers. If I write my own bindings for ALT versions, I can use either old-style exec strings or new-style helpers.\n\nThe most faithful + maintainable approach: my bindings.lua will:\n1. Unbind each default SUPER binding.\n2. Bind the ALT equivalent using hl.dsp helpers (mirroring the defaults).\n\nThis is essentially what bindings-override.conf did. Given ~150 bindings, this is large but mechanical. The user's bindings-override.conf already did this mapping (unbind SUPER X, bindd ALT X). I'll port it line by line, using modern dispatchers where reasonable.\n\nLet me carefully build the mapping from the old override file to modern Lua. I have both the override conf (full text, 315 lines) and the default binding catalog (all files). The override conf covers:\n\nTiling/window ops (from tiling.lua):\n- C (copy), V (paste), X (cut), CTRL+V (clipboard mgr)\n- W (close), CTRL+ALT+DELETE → CTRL+SUPER+DELETE (close all)\n- J (togglesplit), P (pseudo), T (togglefloat), F (fullscreen 0), CTRL+F (tiled fullscreen)\n- O (pop), L (layout toggle)\n- LEFT/RIGHT/UP/DOWN (movefocus)\n- code:10-19 (workspace switch), SHIFT+code:10-19 (move to ws)\n- S (scratchpad), TAB (next ws), SHIFT+TAB (prev), CTRL+TAB (former)\n- SHIFT+arrows (swap)\n- ALT+TAB → SUPER+TAB (cycle next), SHIFT (prev), bring to top (dup)\n- CTRL+ALT+TAB → CTRL+SUPER+TAB (monitor focus)\n- code:20/21 resize, SHIFT variants\n- mouse_down/up workspace scroll\n- G (togglegroup), CTRL+LEFT/RIGHT (group focus)\n- code:61 (monitor scaling cycle) — note: default now uses SUPER+SLASH for scaling up, and SUPER+ALT+SLASH for down; old override used code:61. Hmm, the old override's code:61 was for the OLD default which used code:61. I'll keep user's binding as-is (ALT+code:61).\n- SPACE (launch apps → omarchy-launch-walker) — but default binds SUPER+SPACE to \"Omarchy menu\". The override swapped to ALT+SPACE launch-apps walker. And bindings.conf binds SUPER+SPACE to launch-app.\n- CTRL+E (emoji), CTRL+C (capture), CTRL+O (toggle menu), CTRL+H (hardware), SHIFT+code:201 (root menu), ESCAPE (system), K (keybindings)\n- SHIFT+SPACE (bar), CTRL+SPACE (background), SHIFT+CTRL+SPACE (theme)\n- BACKSPACE variants (transparency, gaps, square aspect)\n- COMMA variants (notifications)\n- CTRL+I (idle), CTRL+N (nightlight), CTRL+Delete (laptop display)\n- PRINT variants: ALT+PRINT → SUPER+PRINT screenrecord; SUPER+PRINT → ALT+PRINT color picker; SUPER+CTRL+PRINT → ALT+CTRL+PRINT OCR\n- CTRL+S (share), CTRL+PERIOD (transcode), CTRL+R (reminder), SHIFT+CTRL+R (clear reminders)\n- CTRL+A (audio), CTRL+B (bluetooth), CTRL+W (wifi), CTRL+T (activity), CTRL+X (dictation), CTRL+Z (zoom in), CTRL+L (lock)\n- bindeld: ALT XF86 volume/brightness precise → SUPER (unbind ALT, bindeld SUPER)\n- bindld: SUPER XF86AudioMute → ALT\n- bindmd: SUPER mouse:272/273 → ALT\n\nPlus bindings.conf custom:\n- ALT+RETURN Terminal (xdg-terminal-exec with cwd)\n- SUPER+ALT+RETURN Tmux\n- ALT+SHIFT+RETURN Browser\n- ALT+SHIFT+F File manager\n- SUPER+ALT+SHIFT+F File manager cwd\n- ALT+SHIFT+B Browser (dup)\n- SUPER+SHIFT+ALT+B Browser private\n- unbind ALT+CTRL+PRINT; ALT+SHIFT+O OCR\n- SUPER mouse_down/up zoom (cursor zoom 1.5x/0.67x), SUPER+SHIFT mouse_down reset\n- SUPER+D wayscriber toggle\n- SUPER+SPACE launch-app\n- unbind ALT+SPACE commented; SUPER+SPACE bindd Launch apps\n\nNote in bindings.conf there's also \"unbind = ALT, PRINT\"? No — that's in override. OK.\n\nNow, how do the override's old dispatchers map to new API? Let me map each:\n\n1. `sendshortcut, CTRL, Insert, activewindow` → the new default uses `universal_clipboard_shortcut(\"CTRL\",\"C\",\"CTRL\",\"Insert\")` for copy. For ALT versions I could just reuse `send_shortcut_once(\"CTRL\", \"Insert\")` — but I can't easily reuse that local function from packaged file... it's local to clipboard.lua. I'd have to write my own. Simpler: `hl.dsp.exec_cmd(\"sendshortcut CTRL Insert activewindow\")`? Need to check exec_cmd syntax — does it take \"dispatcher args\" old-style? Let me check how exec_cmd works in Hyprland 0.56 API.\n\nLet me look at the Hyprland Lua API for exec_cmd. Actually the utilities.lua example: `hl.dsp.exec_cmd(\"omarchy-capture-region --take-window\")` — that's for exec dispatcher presumably, mapping \"exec ...\". Hmm, exec_cmd might take \"dispatcher args...\" full string. Let me check the Hyprland source/docs for the lua API. Hyprland 0.56 has hyprlang lua bindings. Let me search online quickly or check for a doc file locally.\n\nActually — simplest robust approach: use `hl.dsp.send_key_state` directly for sendshortcut equivalents, or write my own universal clipboard helper in my bindings.lua (copying the pattern). That's self-contained and works.\n\nBut wait — maybe I'm overcomplicating. Check the Hyprland Lua API docs to see what hl.dsp.exec_cmd accepts. Let me search the web for \"Hyprland Lua API exec_cmd dispatcher\". Actually the shipped examples show exec_cmd(\"command\") → exec dispatcher. But `hl.dsp.focus(...)` etc. exist for typed dispatchers. Is there a generic dispatcher function? Maybe hl.dsp.exec_cmd only wraps \"exec\". Let me look at Hyprland's lua dsp docs.\n\nLet me quickly check with a web search, since this API is new and I must not guess.",
