he SUPER bindings using a wrapper that intercepts... no, can't intercept packaged file.\n\nAlternative: since `o.bind` is a global function defined in helpers.lua loaded BEFORE the defaults, and the defaults call `o.bind(...)` — if I set `omarchy_default_bindings` to... no, that disables all.\n\nOK here's a genuinely clean approach using a pre-bound wrapper:\n\nIn my bindings.lua I can't affect packaged files (they load first). BUT — what if I redefine `o.bind` and `hl.bind` BEFORE require(\"default.hypr.omarchy\")? I'd have to edit hyprland.lua (in dotfiles, symlinked). The wrapper would translate \"SUPER + ...\" (without ALT) → \"ALT + ...\" and record the original SUPER keys for unbinding. That's elegant:\n\n```lua\n-- in hyprland.lua before require(\"default.hypr.omarchy\")\nlocal original_hl_bind = hl.bind\nlocal swapped = {}\nhl.bind = function(keys, dispatcher, opts)\n  local new_keys = keys\n  if not keys:match(\"ALT\") and keys:match(\"SUPER\") then\n    new_keys = keys:gsub(\"SUPER\", \"ALT\")\n    table.insert(swapped, { super = keys, alt = new_keys })\n  end\n  return original_hl_bind(new_keys, dispatcher, opts)\nend\n```\n\nHmm wait, but there's a problem: `hl.bind` used inside o.bind. Also the swap should NOT apply to... let me check: are there SUPER binds that should stay SUPER? Yes! The user's bindings.conf explicitly uses SUPER: SUPER+SPACE launch-app, SUPER+mouse zoom, SUPER+D wayscriber, SUPER+ALT+RETURN tmux, SUPER+SHIFT+ALT+B private browser, SUPER+SHIFT+ALT+F file manager cwd. But those are in MY file, which loads after defaults. So if the wrapper is only active during the defaults load, my own SUPER binds stay SUPER. \n\nBut also careful: the wrapper would swap SUPER→ALT for everything in defaults, including SUPER+ALT+X combos? No — my condition excludes keys containing ALT, so SUPER+ALT+F stays SUPER+ALT+F. And SUPER+SHIFT+ALT+... stays. But wait — the user's old setup: did SUPER+ALT binds stay SUPER+ALT? Let me check the override again... Th