ict? Default has \"SUPER + CTRL + D\" (Display panel) and \"SUPER + SHIFT + D\" (Docker) — after wrapper: \"ALT + CTRL + D\", \"ALT + SHIFT + D\". Plain \"SUPER + D\" is free. ✓\n\nNow — is the wrapper approach acceptable to the user? It's elegant but somewhat \"clever\"/magic. The alternative is the explicit table (like the override file — ~150 lines). The user's original was an explicit override file. A wrapper in hyprland.lua keeps bindings.lua clean and auto-survives omarchy updates (any new SUPER default binding automatically gets swapped). That's a big maintainability win. But \"magic\" can confuse.\n\nHmm, but there's a risk with the wrapper: `hl.bind` might be called by Hyprland internals or the wrapper could break `hl.define_submap` or the selection-layer binds. The wrapper only wraps hl.bind (and I should keep original reference for internal use). The packaged code uses hl.bind directly too (utilities.lua selection binds use hl.bind directly) — those would get swapped too, but they're RETURN/TAB etc. (no SUPER), so unaffected. Fine.\n\nAlso `o.bind` calls `hl.bind` internally (helpers.lua line 94) — so o.bind calls go through the wrapper. Good, that's exactly what we want.\n\nBUT: there's a subtlety — the wrapper must be installed BEFORE `require(\"default.hypr.omarchy\")` runs. That means editing `hyprland.lua` in dotfiles. The local hyprland.lua is a file created Aug 14 (not symlinked). I need to create a dotfiles hyprland.lua with the wrapper + user overrides and symlink it too.\n\nWait, actually — do I even need the wrapper in hyprland.lua? Alternative: in bindings.lua (loaded after defaults), I can't unbind programmatically without enumerating. But I CAN enumerate all current bindings? Hmm, there's no API to enumerate keybinds in Lua AFAIK (hyprctl bindings exists as a command: `hyprctl bindings`). So I could shell out: get all SUPER binds via `hyprctl bindings -j`, unbind each, rebind ALT with... but rebinding needs dispatchers, which I can't recover