PER? Let me check toggles/flags.lua etc. Earlier grep of toggles dir showed flags.lua, single-window-aspect-ratio.lua, window-no-gaps.lua. If they use o.bind with SUPER, they'd stay SUPER (after restore) — inconsistent with ALT swap, but they're toggle-only binds; check later.\n\nActually hold on. Let me reconsider the whole approach. Is modifying hyprland.lua + wrapper \"magic\" acceptable? An alternative that's more explicit and closer to the user's original: bindings.lua with explicit `hl.unbind(\"SUPER + X\")` + `o.bind(\"ALT + X\", ...)` for every binding. ~150 lines, mechanical, no magic, but breaks when omarchy adds new defaults (they'd silently stay SUPER).\n\nThe user chose \"Full ALT swap (Recommended)\" whose description said: \"unbind every SUPER default, rebind with ALT\". The wrapper does exactly that. I'll go with the wrapper but ALSO handle the post-default explicit adjustments for: PRINT trio, volume precise, audio switch, zoom mouse, tmux, etc.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider once more. There's a real risk the wrapper breaks something subtle (e.g., if hyprland.lua reloads and o.bind is used by toggles before restore...). Also there's `omarchy-menu-keybindings` — it reads bindings via hyprctl, descriptions preserved. Fine.\n\nActually, you know what? Let me look at how the quattro migration script handled the user's old override — there might be an official mechanism. Check the most recent migration (1786643346.sh is the latest). Also there might be a `bindings-override.lua` concept. Let me check what the latest migration does and whether omarchy officially supports a modifier swap.\n\nLet me look at the recent migration script for quattro upgrade.",
